    - ONE browser check drives the runway radio — a control living OUTSIDE the settings form, associated only by `form="settings-form"` — and proves it produces the identical pass/fail contract a natively-nested control does
    - The same check proves the failure half: when the save fails, the runway path surfaces the failure exactly as a nested control does
    - ONE source-level check pins that the runway radios genuinely carry the `form=` attribute and genuinely sit outside the form element
  </behavior>
  <read_first>
    - companion/test_browser_ux.py: 27-04's auto-save on-disk check and its failure counterpart — the "same pass/fail contract" this check must reproduce for a different control
    - companion/test_browser_ux.py: `_commit_field()`, `_wait_for_save_status()`, `_persist_without_js()`/`_persist_once()` (for the on-disk read-back), and `_click_control()` INCLUDING its docstring about coordinate clicks not landing reliably
    - companion/test_browser_ux.py: 28-06's Check B and Check C, so this check reuses their inducement and recovery idioms instead of inventing a third
    - companion/pages/config_page.py: `runway_fieldset()`'s `form=` emission and the comments recording the card's position relative to the settings form
    - companion/static/dirty-state.js: the delegated-listener comment explaining the cross-tree case
  </read_first>
  <action>
    Add ONE browser check — e.g.
    `_a_form_associated_runway_radio_meets_the_same_save_contract_as_a_nested_control()`.

    **Prove the association is real before relying on it.** In the same check, first
    assert from the DOM that the runway radio's `.form` property resolves to the settings
    form AND that the radio is NOT a descendant of it (`form.contains(radio) === false`).
    Without that clause the check could pass on a refactor that quietly nested the control,
    and would then be proving nothing about the `form=` path at all.

    **The success half.** Change the runway selection and assert, as ONE fact: the status
    region held the saving word and then the saved word (in that order — a region that only
    ever shows the end state cannot be distinguished from one that lies), and the new
    runway value is READ BACK OFF DISK. Reuse the existing on-disk read-back step rather
    than writing a second reader.

    **The failure half.** Induce a genuine save failure using the same inducement 28-06's
    Check B uses, with the runway as the control being changed, and assert: the retry
    affordance is visible, the status does NOT hold the saved word, and the runway value
    is NOT on disk. This is what "the same pass/fail contract every other control meets"
    means — both halves, not just the happy one.

    **Drive the radio honestly.** `_click_control()`'s own docstring records that a real
    coordinate click does not land reliably on these controls, and CFG-74's requirement
    text cites that as part of why this harness cannot see the reported Safari failure.
    Use whichever driving method actually commits a real `change` event, and STATE IN THE
    CHECK'S DESCRIPTION which method was used and why — so a future reader knows exactly
    what was and was not exercised.

    **The source check** in `test_config_page.py`: assert the rendered Display page emits
    `form="settings-form"` on every runway radio, and that the runway fieldset's markup
    appears outside the settings form element's own boundaries in the rendered output.

    MUTATION-TEST and QUOTE EVERY MESSAGE VERBATIM:
    - M-A: remove the `form=` attribute from the runway radios. Both the source check and
      the browser check's association clause must fail.
    - M-B: narrow `dirty-state.js`'s delegated listener from `document` to the form
      element itself. The browser check's SUCCESS half must fail — this is the load-
      bearing mutation, because it is exactly the regression the delegated listener exists
      to prevent, and it would leave every natively-nested control still working.
    - M-C: make the failure half's inducement silently succeed. The failure half must
      fail, proving it asserts absence-from-disk rather than merely "something happened".

    Revert every mutation with `git checkout-index -f -- <path>`, never `git checkout --`.
  </action>
  <verify>
    <automated>PY=/home/user/skypane/server/.venv/bin/python; [ -x "$PY" ] || PY=$(command -v python3); "$PY" companion/test_config_page.py && "$PY" companion/test_browser_ux.py</automated>
  </verify>
  <acceptance_criteria>
    - The check asserts BOTH `radio.form === settingsForm` AND `settingsForm.contains(radio) === false`
    - The success half asserts the saving word BEFORE the saved word, and the value ON DISK
    - The failure half asserts the retry affordance visible, the saved word absent, and the value NOT on disk
    - The driving method for the radio is named in the check's registered description with its reason
    - The `test_config_page.py` source check asserts `form="settings-form"` on every runway radio and the fieldset's position outside the form
    - M-A, M-B and M-C failure messages QUOTED VERBATIM; M-B demonstrably fails the success half while nested controls still pass
    - Every mutation reverted with `git checkout-index -f --`; `git status --porcelain` clean at the end
    - `git diff --stat` names no file under `companion/` other than the two harnesses
    - `ruff check .` clean
  </acceptance_criteria>
  <done>The one control that reaches the settings form through an attribute instead of through the DOM tree now has its own proof, on both sides of the contract.</done>
