<task type="auto" tdd="false">
  <name>Task 2: The toast is proven reachable, not merely present</name>
  <files>companion/test_browser_ux.py</files>
  <behavior>
    - ONE check proves that on a genuine save failure the toast is actually VISIBLE to a reader: in the viewport, resolved opacity 1, and not covered by any other element at its own centre
    - It proves this at 360 px and at desktop width, in both themes
    - It proves the toast returns to its invisible rest state afterwards without leaving the accessibility tree
  </behavior>
  <read_first>
    - companion/static/style.css: `.quick-toast` and `.quick-toast.is-visible` in full, INCLUDING the comment explaining why the element is kept in the accessibility tree with `opacity: 0` + `pointer-events: none` rather than `display: none`
    - companion/static/quick-switch.js: `announceFailure()` and `TOAST_DISMISS_MS` — the timer that returns it to rest
    - companion/test_browser_ux.py: 28-06's Check B, which already asserts the toast's COPY — this check asserts its REACHABILITY and must not duplicate the copy assertion
    - companion/test_browser_ux.py: 28-06's Check A, for the `elementFromPoint` non-occlusion idiom being reused here
    - .planning/phases/28-.../28-CONTEXT.md: the CFG-74 paragraph recording that the toast was read and found `position: fixed` with no transformed ancestor — this check is the verification of that reading, not a restatement of it
  </read_first>
  <action>
    Add ONE check — e.g. `_the_failure_toast_is_actually_reachable_when_a_save_fails()`.

    Induce a genuine save failure (reuse 28-06's inducement). Then assert, while the toast
    is up:
    (a) its `getBoundingClientRect()` lies within the viewport — top, bottom, left and
        right all inside, not merely intersecting;
    (b) its RESOLVED `getComputedStyle().opacity` is 1 — the rest state is `opacity: 0`,
        so presence in the DOM proves nothing and a check that asserts presence would pass
        on a toast nobody can see;
    (c) `document.elementFromPoint()` at the toast's own centre resolves to the toast or a
        descendant of it — nothing is covering it;
    (d) no ancestor of the toast has a non-`none` resolved `transform` — a transformed
        ancestor re-parents a `position: fixed` element to that ancestor's box and is one
        of the classic ways a fixed toast ends up off-screen. The deep-dive looked for this
        by reading; this clause looks for it by measuring.

    Then wait out the dismiss timer and assert the toast has returned to resolved opacity
    0 while STILL being present in the DOM and not `display: none` — the accessibility-tree
    property its own CSS comment argues for.

    Run at 360 px and at desktop width, in both themes.

    Do NOT re-assert the toast's copy — 28-06's Check B owns that clause, and duplicating
    it would mean two checks failing for one cause with two different messages.

    MUTATION-TEST and QUOTE EVERY MESSAGE VERBATIM:
    - M-A: set `.quick-toast { top: -400px; }`. Clause (a) must fail.
    - M-B: leave the toast at `opacity: 0` on failure (drop the `is-visible` class). Clause
      (b) must fail — and state explicitly whether a naive "the toast element exists"
      assertion would have passed, because that is the exact failure shape this check was
      written to rule out.
    - M-C: add a `transform: translateZ(0)` to an ancestor of the toast. State the outcome
      of clauses (a), (c) and (d) — if clause (d) is the only one that catches it, that is
      the finding, and it is worth recording precisely.

    Revert every mutation with `git checkout-index -f -- <path>`, never `git checkout --`.

    **Overclaim ban.** This check proves the toast is reachable IN CHROMIUM. Nothing about
    it may be described as proving anything about Safari. Say so in the check's registered
    description.
  </action>
  <verify>
    <automated>PY=/home/user/skypane/server/.venv/bin/python; [ -x "$PY" ] || PY=$(command -v python3); "$PY" companion/test_browser_ux.py</automated>
  </verify>
  <acceptance_criteria>
    - All four clauses (a)-(d) are asserted while the toast is up, at 360 px AND desktop, in both themes
    - Clause (b) asserts RESOLVED opacity 1 — `grep` shows no assertion that merely tests for the element's presence
    - Clause (d) walks the ancestor chain and asserts every resolved `transform` is `none`
    - The post-dismiss state is asserted as resolved opacity 0 AND still present AND not `display: none`
    - The toast's COPY is NOT re-asserted here — 28-06's Check B owns it
    - M-A and M-B quoted; M-B states explicitly whether a presence-only assertion would have passed; M-C's clause-by-clause outcome recorded
    - Every mutation reverted with `git checkout-index -f --`; `git status --porcelain` clean at the end
    - The check's registered description states the Chromium-only limitation
    - `ruff check .` clean
  </acceptance_criteria>
  <done>The failure toast is now measured as visible rather than read as probably-visible, including the transformed-ancestor trap that a code read cannot rule out.</done>
